# DuDuClaw gateway -- Yocto bring-up unit.
#
# Adapted from appliance/mkosi.extra/etc/systemd/system/duduclaw-gateway.service
# (the Debian appliance line's verified unit), simplified the same way
# duduclaw-sysd.service was for this Y2-1 bring-up stage:
#   - No User=duduclaw / ReadWritePaths=/data -- this Yocto image doesn't
#     provision a non-root service user or a /data mount yet (that's
#     product-layer work for a later ticket). Runs as root for now.
#   - No After=/Wants=/Environment=DUDUCLAW_HOME on/for
#     duduclaw-firstboot-provision.service in THIS base unit -- STALE CLAIM
#     CORRECTED (WP3, 2026-08-29, DESIGN-installer-settings-integration-
#     2026-08.md): this bullet used to say those Debian-line concepts were
#     "not yet ported to the Yocto product layer", which is no longer true.
#     duduclaw-firstboot-provision.service DOES exist as a Yocto concept
#     since Y9-1 (recipes-duduclaw/duduclaw-firstboot/), and
#     Environment=DUDUCLAW_HOME=/data/duduclaw IS already wired on every
#     Yocto image that actually has a /data partition (duduclaw-image-data.bb,
#     -flatpak.bb, -ab.bb, and -appliance.bb -- the actual shipping target)
#     -- just not by editing THIS file. Two systemd drop-ins do it instead,
#     each installed only by the recipe that owns the feature and only onto
#     the image that also carries the matching /data partition:
#     recipes-duduclaw/duduclaw-ab-update/files/10-ab-home.conf and
#     recipes-duduclaw/duduclaw-firstboot/files/10-data.conf (both under
#     duduclaw-gateway.service.d/). 10-data.conf's own header already gives
#     the reason this base unit stays untouched: it is shared, unmodified,
#     by every image that installs duduclaw-cli -- including THIS bring-up
#     image (duduclaw-image.bb itself), which still has no /data partition
#     or duduclaw-firstboot recipe at all -- so a Requires= baked in here
#     would fail that image's boot outright (Requires= on a unit whose
#     .service file was never installed does not resolve, unlike a merely-
#     unresolvable Wants=/After=). Re-verified this round (WP3): no
#     .bbappend anywhere under meta-duduclaw overrides this file (grepped).
#     Net effect for WP3's own goal -- the gateway reading
#     /data/duduclaw/pending-account.json on first boot,
#     crates/duduclaw-gateway/src/pending_account.rs -- is already satisfied
#     on every image the graphical installer actually targets; this base
#     file is deliberately left without those two directives rather than
#     re-adding, for a weaker (ordering-only) directive, the exact
#     base-unit coupling 10-data.conf's own header already reasoned against
#     and rejected once for the stronger (Requires=) one.
#   - Kept: After=duduclaw-sysd.service ordering (the gateway talks to sysd
#     over a Unix socket, matches the appliance unit's own reasoning), and
#     the Type=notify + WatchdogSec=60 contract (the app-side sd_notify
#     ping already lives in crates/duduclaw-gateway/src/watchdog.rs,
#     unrelated to which distro packages the binary).
#   - Deliberately NOT ordered after network-online.target, same reasoning
#     as the appliance unit: the gateway binds 0.0.0.0 and reconnects its
#     own channels/relay with backoff, so waiting for "fully online" only
#     stalls boot.
#
# `duduclaw run --yes` is the same invocation the appliance line uses --
# `duduclaw` is the one binary this recipe (duduclaw-cli) produces, and
# `run` is its gateway-server subcommand (see crates/duduclaw-cli/src/bin/
# duduclaw.rs) -- this is the concrete evidence that "gateway" and "cli" in
# the Y2-1 task brief are the same artifact wearing two hats.
#
# Y5-4 (2026-08-26) real fix, caught by a live QEMU boot, not by re-reading
# this file: the "the gateway binds 0.0.0.0" comment two paragraphs up was
# WRONG for this unit specifically -- `crates/duduclaw-core/src/appliance.rs`
# `pick_default_bind()` only defaults to `0.0.0.0` when `DUDUCLAW_APPLIANCE=1`
# is set in the process environment; with no env and no `config.toml
# [gateway] bind` (this bring-up image still has neither -- WP3's
# correction above is that duduclaw-firstboot-provision.service now exists
# as a Yocto concept elsewhere, not that THIS image installs it), the
# code default is `127.0.0.1` -- confirmed live: `journalctl -u
# duduclaw-gateway` showed "Gateway starting on 127.0.0.1:18789", and a host
# -> guest connection (QEMU hostfwd, which relays to the guest's real NIC,
# never its loopback) got "Connection reset by peer" every time. The Debian
# appliance line never hits this because its own unit
# (appliance/mkosi.extra/etc/systemd/system/duduclaw-gateway.service) sets
# this exact env var already; this Yocto unit's own comment above just
# asserted the resulting BEHAVIOR without asserting the CAUSE. Setting it
# here is not a workaround for a security decision -- Yocto Y1-1/Y3-x bring-
# up simply never carried it over, and a screenless appliance whose
# dashboard cannot be reached from the LAN (or, for VM testing, from the
# host) fails this whole image's stated purpose the same way a black kiosk
# screen does.
#
# `DUDUCLAW_BIND=0.0.0.0` is ALSO set explicitly, not left to
# `DUDUCLAW_APPLIANCE=1`'s default-tier side effect alone:
# `resolve_gateway_bind`'s priority is `DUDUCLAW_BIND` env > `config.toml
# [gateway] bind` > the `DUDUCLAW_APPLIANCE`-gated default, and the FIRST
# `duduclaw run --yes` on a fresh image persists whatever it resolved into
# `config.toml` -- so on any machine that already booted once before this
# fix lands (e.g. a VM disk carried over from an earlier image build),
# `config.toml` may already pin `bind = "127.0.0.1"` from that boot, which
# would silently outrank `DUDUCLAW_APPLIANCE=1` from then on (reproduced
# live on this exact VM). The explicit env var wins unconditionally and
# needs no `config.toml` migration.
[Unit]
Description=DuDuClaw gateway (Yocto bring-up)
After=duduclaw-sysd.service
Wants=duduclaw-sysd.service
StartLimitIntervalSec=300
StartLimitBurst=5

[Service]
Type=notify
NotifyAccess=main
Environment=DUDUCLAW_APPLIANCE=1
Environment=DUDUCLAW_BIND=0.0.0.0
ExecStart=/usr/bin/duduclaw run --yes
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5
WatchdogSec=60

# WS-3/A2 (2026-09-01, DESIGN-os-security-line-2026-09.md §2 支柱一 A2 /
# G11): first systemd sandboxing pass on the gateway unit. Still runs as
# root (User=root is NOT added here -- A3/G12's own service-user work is a
# separate, larger ticket per that design doc's own scoping: "涉及 /data
# 權限遷移與 spawn 鏈，是 P0 裡最大的一件，可獨立票"), so these four
# directives only remove privilege-escalation and filesystem-write surface
# that this root process does not actually need, they do not attempt to
# drop uid 0 itself.
#
# NoNewPrivileges=yes: blocks setuid/setgid/file-capability escalation on
# every exec() this unit or its children perform (systemd.exec(5)). Since
# the unit already runs as uid 0, this cannot "lose" any privilege the
# process already has (exec'ing a setuid-root helper as root grants no NEW
# privilege) -- it only closes the escalation path an attacker inside this
# process would otherwise have via a setuid/setcap binary reachable on
# $PATH. No functional dependency found on privilege escalation via exec:
# this unit spawns duduclaw's own subprocess tree (agent CLI runtimes,
# office-doc converters, compat-runner wrappers) as plain children, and the
# one component that DOES need privilege separation
# (recipes-duduclaw/duduclaw-sysd/files/duduclaw-sysd.service) is already a
# SEPARATE unit reached over /run/duduclaw/sysd.sock precisely so this unit
# never has to escalate itself.
#
# ProtectHome=read-only: makes /home, /root, and /run/user read-only for
# this unit. Verified against the actual DUDUCLAW_HOME resolution before
# adding this (crates/duduclaw-core/src/platform.rs:38-40 -- precedence is
# `$DUDUCLAW_HOME` verbatim, else `<home_dir()>/.duduclaw`, i.e. `/root/
# .duduclaw` for this root-run unit with no override): every image that
# actually ships or is QEMU-verified today already overrides DUDUCLAW_HOME
# away from /root via a drop-in under this unit's .d/ directory --
# recipes-duduclaw/duduclaw-ab-update/files/10-ab-home.conf (duduclaw-
# image-ab.bb, required by -appliance.bb) and recipes-duduclaw/
# duduclaw-firstboot/files/10-data.conf (duduclaw-image-data.bb /
# -flatpak.bb / -appliance.bb, same value) both set
# `Environment=DUDUCLAW_HOME=/data/duduclaw` -- a top-level mountpoint
# entirely outside /home and /root, so ProtectHome=read-only never touches
# it on any of those images. The one exception is the bare, standalone
# duduclaw-image.bb (no drop-in, no /data partition) falling back to
# /root/.duduclaw -- that recipe is the superseded Y2-1 bring-up milestone,
# not part of the current shipping/verification chain (release-os.sh's
# DEFAULT_IMAGE is duduclaw-image-appliance; the QEMU harnesses target
# -appliance/-appliance-test), so this is a known, accepted, documented
# trade-off rather than a silently-assumed one -- flagged here rather than
# worked around, since working around it would mean weakening the
# hardening for every image that DOES have /data just to keep a superseded
# milestone recipe's own standalone build fully functional.
#
# PrivateTmp=yes: isolates /tmp and /var/tmp into a private mount namespace
# for this unit (and its children, which inherit the same namespace on
# fork/exec -- so agent subprocesses spawned by the gateway see a
# consistent, shared private /tmp among themselves, just not the host's
# real /tmp). Checked for cross-process /tmp handoff before adding this (no
# such pattern found): the gateway's actual cross-process IPC surfaces are
# `/run/duduclaw/sysd.sock` (duduclaw-sysd, RuntimeDirectory=duduclaw) and
# `/run/duduclaw-kiosk/duduclaw-shell.sock` (duduclaw-comp's shell_control,
# crates/duduclaw-gateway/src/display_bridge.rs's own KIOSK_RUNTIME_DIR
# constant) -- both under /run, untouched by PrivateTmp. The Docker-backed
# agent container sandbox (crates/duduclaw-container/src/docker.rs) also
# does not hand the host's /tmp to containers: its own `/tmp` mount is a
# fresh in-container tmpfs (`tmpfs.insert("/tmp", "rw,noexec,nosuid,
# size=64m")`), and its host-side bind mounts are agent workspace/`/run/
# duduclaw` paths, never `/tmp`. Every `std::env::temp_dir()` call actually
# reached at runtime (secaudit report staging, OAuth PKCE redirect files,
# skill-install staging, updater scratch space) is per-process private
# scratch consumed by the same process before or after use, not a file
# dropped for a DIFFERENT systemd unit to pick up.
#
# ProtectSystem=full: /usr, /boot, and /etc become read-only for this
# unit. Deliberately "full", not "strict" (design doc: "保守起步，strict
# 留 P1") -- "strict" would make the ENTIRE filesystem read-only by default
# and require enumerating every writable path via ReadWritePaths=, which is
# real design work (P1's read-only-root/overlay ticket, G3) this P0 pass
# does not attempt. Under "full" specifically, /data is untouched (it is
# not /usr, /boot, or /etc, and is not covered by ProtectHome either) --
# ReadWritePaths=-/data below is therefore not load-bearing under "full"
# today; it is added now, with the `-` (systemd.exec(5): ignore if the
# path does not exist) prefix so it is a harmless no-op on the bare
# duduclaw-image.bb build that has no /data partition at all, specifically
# so this line does not need to be revisited the moment a future ticket
# tightens ProtectSystem to "strict" (at which point it stops being inert
# and starts being required) -- documented here rather than silently
# deferred.
NoNewPrivileges=yes
ProtectHome=read-only
PrivateTmp=yes
ProtectSystem=full
ReadWritePaths=-/data

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
